Why computers?
• A mathematical question initially: is there a mechanical way – i.e., a clearly specified sequence of instructions, each one with predictable results – to decide the veracity of a formal statement? • David Hilbert (who asked the question): no. • Alan Turing: on the other hand, if the statement is decidable, it can be computed by a machine. • Since then, a syllogism with two unproven hypotheses: • P1: any computable function can be by a Turing machine (Church-Turing thesis) • P2: human reasoning is made of computable functions (Putnam and Fodor functionalist-computationalist thesis) • C: we can reason using a Turing machine • Computers are a Turing machine by their structure • Arithmetic and logic unit, memory, input/outputs
