Application to drug discovery (with Fathi Souaker)
 Drug discovery involves several costly and time-consuming steps:
1. Identification of the therapeutic target (bacteria, virus, tissue, hormone, etc.)
2. Inventory of the molecules that can act on the target (hit generation)
3. Prospect isolation based on biophysical, chemical and industrial criteria
4. Biochemical optimisation to design derivatives and isomers that meet the maximum number of criteria (40% of the R&D costs).
5. In vivo testing on animals for toxicity and pharmacology qualification.
6. Clinical trials on human upon successful step 5.
The machine learning connection
 Can automate tasks such as drug-target interaction, drug-drug
interaction, compound screening, quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR), etc.
 The automatic identification of prospect molecules saves time and money
 One-shot classification can reach the goal without need of retraining the network for new classes (thanks to meta-learning)
 Assesses the similarity between the structure of candidate molecules and a proven reference
